        "description": "Skull trophy of the Bunum people, which lacks the lower jaw; It retains a molar at each end of the upper jaw. It is inside a hemp net bag with two handles and rope to close it. The skull belongs to a male adult. Although age is difficult to determine, it presents obliteration of coronal and sagittal sutures, indicative of a fairly advanced age. He has severe dental wear and abscesses in the right M1 and appears to have alveolar re-absorption in M2. The wear of the teeth is associated with age and diet. There is no intentional head deformation, but it seems that he suffered a nose trauma. It is part of the temporary exhibition Cut heads. Symbols of power in the Museum of Prehistory of Valencia. Bunum culture, Taiwan. XIX century. Bag: Height: 34 cm; width: 38 cm Skull: Height: 14.70 cm; width: 13.40 cm; depth: 19.10 cm Inv. No. CE2184. National Museum of Anthropology of Spain.",
